+# Specialty Directory Derived in Memory from the Cached Veterinarian List
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-SPEC-001` publishes the veterinarian directory the other way round: every specialty the clinic knows, with the veterinarians holding it.
+
+Four facts of the existing code shape the answer. The many-to-many association is mapped on `Vet` only, so no inverse navigation from `Specialty` exists. No repository for specialties exists. A specialty no veterinarian holds must still be listed, so the veterinarian roster alone cannot answer the page. `BaseEntity` defines neither `equals` nor `hashCode`, so two reads of the same stored row are unequal objects.
+
+The PRD requires a stable presentation order and leaves the sort key open (2026-08-14).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Group the veterinarians in the controller.** Rejected: a specialty no veterinarian holds never appears, and the grouping becomes a controller rule the pattern catalog's *Web controller* row excludes.
+2. **A join query returning each specialty with its holders.** Rejected: it adds a second read path over data the `vets` cache already holds, and it splits the two orderings between query and page.
+3. **Compose the page from a specialty read and the cached veterinarian read, grouping in a value object** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+A `SpecialtyRepository` reads the specialties. `VetRepository.findAll()` supplies the veterinarians from the existing cache. A `SpecialtyDirectory` value object pairs each specialty with its holders from those two collections.
+
+Holders match specialties by persisted identity, because the entities carry no value equality.
+
+Specialties are ordered by name, and the holders under each by last name, then first name, then identity. This settles the PRD's open ordering question at design time, and the product owner may revisit it.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The grouping rule and both orderings live in one type, testable with real objects and no database.
+- The page adds one query for specialties and reuses the cached veterinarian read.
+- The controller binds nothing, delegates, and selects a view, so no new rule lands in a controller.
+- The whole roster is loaded per request, as the `/vets` route already does.
+- The directory inherits the veterinarian cache's unsettled invalidation question, recorded in [system-design.md](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey).
+- Ordering departs from the `PetTypeRepository` precedent, which orders in the query. The shared, cached veterinarian read admits no per-caller ordering.
+- Two stored specialties sharing a name appear as two entries.
+- The page exposes specialty names and veterinarian names to every caller, as the veterinarian directory already publishes both and no authentication exists.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-SPEC-001
+
+- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/SpecialtyDirectory.java`
+- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/SpecialtyRepository.java`
+- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/SpecialtyController.java`
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the types this decision introduces.
+- [Feature-Package Organization Without a Service Layer](2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md) — the package the directory joins.
